//? Title: Matrix-chain ordering
//? Subtitle: Actual interval decisions, arithmetic costs and representable optima
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Multiplying compatible matrices in different orders can change the number of
scalar multiplications while producing the same mathematical product. We choose
a minimum-cost binary parenthesization, show actual interval decisions on three
matrices, retain the original six-matrix example, and distinguish arithmetic
planning from matrix execution. Positive dimensions and explicit overflow
handling make the cost model checkable.
)

\toc

> Derive the interval recurrence

Matrix Ai has dimensions d[i−1] by d[i]. A final split k of the interval i..j
first forms i..k and k+1..j, then multiplies a d[i−1]-by-d[k] matrix by a
d[k]-by-d[j] matrix. The last product costs d[i−1]*d[k]*d[j] scalar
multiplications under the conventional dense arithmetic model. Addition counts,
sparsity, hardware packing and numerical roundoff are outside this objective.

$$
m[i,j]=\min_{i\le k<j}\{m[i,k]+m[k+1,j]+d[i-1]d[k]d[j]\},\qquad m[i,i]=0.
$$
\alttext(The cost of an interval is the smallest sum of its two child costs and the scalar cost of their final multiplication.)

Every binary parenthesization has exactly one final split. Replacing either
child by a cheaper child cannot increase its dimensions, so optimal children
give an optimal parent. Induction on interval length proves the recurrence. Fill
shorter intervals first; examine starts and splits in increasing order. A strict
improvement rule preserves the first minimizing split, including equal-cost
ties. This rule chooses one optimum rather than enumerating all optima.

> Three matrices: read the actual decisions

For dimensions10,30,5,60, multiplying A1A2 first costs1500, then3000,
giving4500. Multiplying A2A3 first costs9000, then18000, giving27000. The
diagram below reads the actual final-interval trial records, so its alternatives
come from the producer rather than a reconstructed illustration.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/matrix_chain_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/matrix_chain.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::matrix_chain_trace({10, 30, 5, 60});
    std::print("| actual i | actual j | actual split | left cost | right cost | final product | candidate " "cost | selected |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (auto &r : t.trials)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", r.i, r.j, r.k,
                   r.left, r.right, r.product, r.cost, r.selected);
    auto &a = t.trials[2];
    auto &b = t.trials[3];
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("a", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual split {}\ncandidate {}", a.k, a.cost)).node(
        "b", {6, 0}, std::format("Actual split {}\ncandidate {}", b.k, b.cost)).node("c", {12, 0}, std::format("Actual chosen interval\ncost {}", t.analysis.result.cost)).edge("a", "b").edge("b", "c");
    d.save_svg("build/matrix_chain_tiny.svg");
}
```
