> Verification and references

Native tests enumerate every dimension sequence in1..3 through six matrices,
independently enumerate full binary parenthesizations, and inspect every actual
trial, commit, strict tie, table entry and quota. Original15125/40500/47500
costs, empty/one-matrix cases, nonpositive dimensions, ownership and
unrepresentable full costs are retained. A deliberately overflowing unused
subchain with a representable full optimum checks saturation semantics. Existing
combinatorial-search regressions remain. The shared ten-report batch passes 71
CTest cases, 29 directly instrumented ASan/UBSan cases, 130 compiled and
executed public examples, 598/598 scoped documentation definitions and ten
representative hovers. Independent Python arbitrary-precision, hashlib,
sorted-window, RNG, polynomial, graph and full-assignment references verify
224,229 actual records. Frozen comparisons preserve ordinary legacy outputs,
including selected clique identities, original density graphs and memetic
partitions. Twelve checkout headers and twelve identically installed headers
compile independently; an installed-header native consumer links and runs. All
four complete programs and all four native figures pass execution and visual
review, and HTML/Markdown exports preserve computed results and identical image
bytes. Receipts: `build/mepml-conversion/batch-224-233/`.

The recurrence is the standard matrix-chain ordering example in Cormen,
Leiserson, Rivest and Stein, *Introduction to Algorithms*.
[MIT Press book page|https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262046305/introduction-to-algorithms/]
identifies the source. This module plans the conventional dense scalar-product
objective; it does not execute, benchmark or guarantee floating-point accuracy
of the selected matrix product.
